// T4 SOUNDNESS gate: a slice must NOT release an element it borrowed.
//
// The companion to arc_release_slice_elem_probe.goo, and the one that matters.
// That probe proves owned elements ARE reclaimed. This one proves borrowed ones
// are NOT, which is the direction that corrupts memory rather than wasting it.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The element-ownership rule was first shipped with unit
// rows and the reclamation probe, and both looked convincing. Then the rule was
// mutated to claim ownership UNCONDITIONALLY and `make arc-release-probe` still
// passed, because nothing in the suite appended a value the CALLER owns. The
// unit rows caught it; the end-to-end gate did not. A gate that cannot go red
// for the defect it exists to catch is not a gate.
//
// THE SHAPE. `collect` appends its PARAMETER, which the caller still owns.
// binding_is_owned refuses a bare identifier, so `p` must not own its elements
// and the release must leave `s` alone. If it does not, `main` reads a freed
// buffer on the next line and valgrind reports an Invalid read.
//
// `owned` is loop-scoped, so condition 4 refuses to release it and the ONLY
// release that could touch that buffer is the unsound one inside collect. That
// isolation is deliberate: it means a failure here has exactly one cause.
//
// THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS PROBE WAS VACUOUS, and it read as convincing. It
// checked `len(owned)` after the call, which loads the LENGTH out of field 1 of
// the {i8*, i64} string and never dereferences the buffer at all -- so it
// passed against the very mutant it was written to catch. The read must touch
// the BYTES, which a concatenation does: goo_string_concat memcpys both
// operands. Verified by re-running it against that mutant.
package main

var sink int

func collect(s string) int {
	p := []string{}
	p = append(p, s)
	return len(p)
}

func main() {
	total := 0
	for i := 0; i < 1000; i++ {
		owned := "abc" + "def"
		total = total + collect(owned)
		// READ THE BYTES after the call. Under a too-permissive element rule
		// the buffer behind `owned` is already free here, and the concat's
		// memcpy walks it.
		echoed := owned + "!"
		sink = sink + len(echoed)
	}
	println(total + sink)
}
